// CARD a0-01 · SEE: the real 1958 patent drawing on a dark desk; the camera rides down from the filing header to FIG. 1; on "today" a plastic brick drops onto the drawn brick and clicks into place · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@patent_snap;doc:lego_doc_patent_us3005282_drawing_sheet1;go:today;fx:.32;fy:.27;h:.055;then:PATENT DRAWING · 1958;now:TODAY;out:right"
LEGO says a brick made in nineteen fifty-eight still snaps onto a brick made **today**.

// CARD a0-02 · SEE: real hands pressing studded bricks together; the camera pushes in; on the last "promise" the clip slows and freezes on the click · SEAM in right, out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_white_bricks_hands;move:in;z:.18;slow:Remember;freeze:promise;in:right;out:right"
That's a promise about precision, kept for more than sixty **years**. Remember that promise.

// CARD a0-03 · SEE: a real pile of LEGO bricks, dim; on "story" bricks start raining in, faster and faster, and pile up from the floor · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@brick_rain;bg:lego_bricks_assorted;go:story;n:170;rate:30;out:right"
This is the story of how a company famous for precision lost count of its own **parts**, and how it got the count back.

// CARD a0-04 · SEE: LEGO's own online shop of June 2004, the camera dollying along it; on "tell" price tags swing in onto the products one by one, every tag reading only "?" · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@price_query;bg:lego_doc_wayback_shop_2004_06_crop;go:tell;tags:.345,.42~.49,.40~.643,.42~.855,.2;lab:WHAT DID EACH BOX COST?;in:right"
{wry} In two thousand and three, LEGO couldn't tell you what its own boxes **cost**. (J)

// CARD a0-05 · SEE: a box lands with its small price tag; on "designers" its parts pour out into a heap and a much bigger tag drops onto the heap · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@box_vs_parts;bg:lego_technic_supra_chassis;go:designers;heap:70;btag:THE BOX'S PRICE;htag:WHAT THE PARTS COST;note:LEGO designer Mark Stafford · 2014 interview · motor and fibre-optic sets;out:right"
One of its designers later said some sets cost more to source than the whole box sold **for**.

// CARD a0-06 · SEE: the camera travels across a real box of sorted LEGO Technic parts, compartment after compartment; on "knew" it drains to grey · SEAM in right, hard cut out
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;under:lego_technic_bits;ua:0;move:right;z:.3;mono:knew;in:right"
And no one at LEGO actually **knew**. (J)

// CARD a0-07 · SEE: red trading screens falling in the dark; the real 2005 news clipping (PBN, carrying Bloomberg) slides in large; on "lost" a red marker underlines "the net loss widened to 1.93 billion kroner" · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@clipping_over_footage;clip:lego_clip_chart_falling_screens;doc:lego_doc_headline_bloomberg_pbn_2005;crop:.21,.245,.6,.37;mark:.22,.351,.59,.364;go:lost;rot:-2;out:right"
The next year, LEGO lost almost two billion **kroner**.

// CARD a0-08 · SEE: the camera travels across LEGO's old System House in Billund; on "Bankers" a yellow note slaps onto the picture and SELL. writes itself in pencil · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@advice_note;pic:lego_system_house;word:SELL.;go:Bankers;x:.66;y:.38;src:the advice, as told in an IE Business School case;in:right"
Bankers advised the family to sell the **company**.

// CARD a0-09 · SEE: a real LEGO city diorama, dim; on "Twenty" a tower of blue (MATTEL) and white (HASBRO) bricks builds itself; on "bigger" a red LEGO tower overtakes it; $9.52B and $10.85B land · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@tower_compare;bg:lego_clip_build_legotown;l:MATTEL^5.38^blue~HASBRO^4.14^white;r:LEGO^10.85^red;step:.7;go:Twenty;rgo:bigger;lab:REVENUE 2024 · US$ BILLIONS (REPORTED);out:right"
{tense} Twenty years later, LEGO was bigger than Mattel and Hasbro **combined**.

// CARD a0-10 · SEE: a man reading a newspaper, dim; on "Everyone" WENT BANKRUPT and SAVED BY A HIT TOY type in; on "never" the first is struck (NO FILING · NO COURT); on "saved" the second is struck · SEAM in right, hard cut out · HOLD
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@myth_strike;clip:lego_clip_newspaper_man;lines:WENT BANKRUPT^never^NO FILING · NO COURT~SAVED BY A HIT TOY^saved;in:right"
So how did LEGO nearly die? Everyone says it nearly went bankrupt, and was saved by going back to the **brick**.
The real story is stranger. It never went bankrupt, and no hit toy saved **it**. [BEAT]

// CARD a0-11 · MOTIF · SEE: LEGO House's bin of red bricks; one brick drops and lands on the white ledge; COUNTING sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_bricks_legohouse_display;go:counting;x:.66;y:.48;h:.13;lab:COUNTING;lx:.42;z:.25;out:right"
It was saved by **counting**.
